Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm trying to use the lme function to run a mixed effects model.

My random effect variable is Animal_ID, my fixed effects are TrialDay, Trt, and the interaction of TrialDay and Trt. The outcome variable is duration of the Behavior, which is currently subset to Latencytoapproach.

Here's how I currently have my model set up:

LA <- lme(duration ~ Trt + TrialDay + Trt*Trialday, random = Animal_ID)

But I'm receiving an error message:

Error in reStruct(random, REML = REML, data = NULL) :

  object 'Animal_ID' not found

What am I doing wrong? Please help! I'm new to mixed models in R.

Thank you,

Rachael

From u05bda608df45c6f4 at groups.google.com  Mon Oct  7 18:59:04 2019
From: u05bda608df45c6f4 at groups.google.com (Evan Eskew)
Date: Mon, 07 Oct 2019 18:59:04 +0000
Subject: Re: [D-RUG]: lme function
Message-ID: <pj3SMM5Wj4k.ZTlEarjVBAAJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <pj3SMM5Wj4k.--RtNw0wCgAJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
References: <pj3SMM5Wj4k.--RtNw0wCgAJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/pj3SMM5Wj4k/m/ZTlEarjVBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Rachael,

There are a few things going on here:

- I believe the function you're looking to use is "lmer()", not "lme()"
- You need to explicitly specify your data within the function call (I'm going to assume your data frame is called "my_data_frame")
- If, as your random effects, you're looking to have varying intercepts by "Animal_ID", you'll need to specify those like so in your model formula: "(1|Animal_ID)"

Thus, I think this should work for you:

LA <- lmer(duration ~ Trt + TrialDay + Trt*Trialday + (1|Animal_ID), data = my_data_frame)

>From there, I'd recommend checking out the example code here: https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/lme4/vignettes/lmer.pdf

All my best,
Evan

